WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.007
Linux systems run processes to perform work.

2
00:00:03.007 --> 00:00:09.020
Some processes are temporary, such as a command a user starts and then closes.

3
00:00:09.020 --> 00:00:18.039
Others are long-running services, often called daemons, that support login, scheduling, logging, networking, databases, web applications, monitoring, backups, or business applications.

4
00:00:18.039 --> 00:00:22.764
Services are normal, but every service deserves a reason to exist.

5
00:00:22.764 --> 00:00:26.629
Some services listen on network ports and accept connections.

6
00:00:26.629 --> 00:00:35.219
That exposure may be required, as with a web service on a web server or SSH on an administration path.

7
00:00:35.219 --> 00:00:39.514
But unnecessary listening services create management burden and potential risk.

8
00:00:39.514 --> 00:00:43.810
The issue is not only whether a port is open.

9
00:00:43.810 --> 00:00:54.118
Administrators should know what software owns the port, why it is reachable, which users or systems need it, how it is updated, and who

10
00:00:54.118 --> 00:00:55.836
is responsible for it.

11
00:00:55.836 --> 00:01:02.708
Processes and services also include scheduled jobs, local agents, monitoring components, backup tools, and application workers.

12
00:01:02.708 --> 00:01:13.016
A defensive review asks what is running, whether it should start automatically, whether it runs with appropriate privilege, where it writes logs, and what

13
00:01:13.016 --> 00:01:15.163
would happen if it stopped.

14
00:01:15.163 --> 00:01:22.036
Unknown services are not automatically malicious, but they are operational unknowns that need ownership and documentation.

15
00:01:22.036 --> 00:01:23.754
Changes should be careful.

16
00:01:23.754 --> 00:01:29.767
Disabling a service without understanding dependencies can break applications, monitoring, remote access, or backups.

17
00:01:29.767 --> 00:01:40.075
A safer approach is to inventory services, confirm purpose with the owner, test in a non-production environment when possible, plan a change window for

18
00:01:40.075 --> 00:01:43.081
important systems, and keep a rollback path.

19
00:01:43.081 --> 00:01:47.376
Reducing exposure is valuable, but it should be done deliberately.
